#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222324} :activity/id #uuid "495fe43b-de66-5798-9669-4b55442f64d4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048881 :topic/id #uuid "c94f64e0-3901-584e-8de4-83ca8b66843b" :topic/math-academy-id 6378} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222494 :tutorial/content "Consider an autonomous system written as $$\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),$$ where $\\mathbf{x}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})=[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}(x,y) \\\\ {f}_{2}(x,y)\\end{aligned}]$.\n\nThe system is called **almost linear** at an equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ if the Jacobian determinant at this point is nonzero there, i.e. $$\\det\\!\\bigl(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\\bigr)\\neq 0.$$\n\nFor example, let $$\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}), \\qquad \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})= [\\begin{aligned}x+y+{y}^{2} \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}], \\qquad \\mathbf{x}^\\ast=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nFirst, notice that $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ so $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium.\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1+2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}$$ Thus, we have\n\n$$J = |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2y \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2(0) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian is $0,$ the system is *not* almost linear at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$." :tutorial/id #uuid "fbbe8bd0-4cef-54d0-9803-a59eee727c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14492 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6570665c-9c0e-5698-9c44-79ef60fe6401" :step/math-academy-id 591480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222325}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222325 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221182 :question/id #uuid "0f4ae74c-67d0-5c0e-99db-e834253b3851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396556}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"} :step/id #uuid "4ea90440-2d8e-5c27-b532-f6be5d45b3b4" :step/math-academy-id 591481 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222326}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222496 :tutorial/content "Suppose the system $$\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),$$ is almost linear at an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ In this case, the system's behavior near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ can be approximated by a linear system.\n\nFirst, we define the deviation from equilibrium by $$\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$$ Small values of $\\mathbf{u}$ correspond to points close to the equilibrium.\n\nNext, assuming $\\mathbf{x} = [x, y]^T,$ we compute the Jacobian matrix $$\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})= [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}],$$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to obtain the constant matrix $J = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast).$\n\nFinally, since $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ the nonlinear system can be approximated by the **linearized system** $$\\mathbf{u}'= J \\mathbf{u}.$$\n\nThis linear system captures the leading-order behavior of solutions close to the equilibrium.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9356e91f-f080-5827-a567-781aedac309a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14494 :tutorial/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "c10be260-d12b-595c-b241-523d377180f4" :step/math-academy-id 591484 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221181 :question/id #uuid "87b69a53-94f5-51e6-9c3a-e403abbf1fa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9aeac73-8ee3-5fef-a47c-a96e3545d89c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050780}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368184}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "264a8b35-ed46-5392-a8ec-e88ead83801a" :step/math-academy-id 591485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222495 :tutorial/content "Again, suppose $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium of the nonlinear system $$\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),$$ and assume the system is *almost linear* at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. If $$J=\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)$$ denotes the Jacobian matrix evaluated at the equilibrium, then the behavior of solutions near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is determined by the eigenvalues of $J.$\n\nThis information can be summarized in the following table.\n\n| **Eigenvalues of the Jacobian** | **Behavior** | **Stability** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| real and both positive | source / unstable node | unstable |\n| real and both negative | sink / stable node | asymptotically stable |\n| real and opposite signs | saddle | unstable |\n| complex with positive real part | spiral source | unstable |\n| complex with negative real part | spiral sink | asymptotically stable |\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8d4acde-0ca3-57a4-9994-a551a9d0094b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14493 :tutorial/title "Stability and Classification of Isolated Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "828feab6-cd8f-5d79-9356-a39ac111b298" :step/math-academy-id 591488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221419 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221183 :question/id #uuid "fd2d2d61-53a6-51c1-811c-41393270ddba" :question/math-academy-id "e-21676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "777fae40-2377-5e63-89f6-5c857beba2b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050967}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385241}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying an Isolated Critical Point of a Nonlinear System"} :step/id #uuid "29c915bc-5c95-5594-b0cf-10edbe6f04a3" :step/math-academy-id 591489 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222497 :tutorial/content "In the classification above, we intentionally skipped one case:\n\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ of the Jacobian at the equilibrium are purely imaginary.\n\nIn this situation, the corresponding linearized system has a *center* at the origin, meaning that solutions neither grow nor decay, but instead circulate around the equilibrium.\n\nFor the original nonlinear system, however, this case is *inconclusive*. While the linearized system predicts closed orbits, the nonlinear system *may* exhibit a stable center, an unstable spiral, or other nearby behavior.\n\nTherefore, when the Jacobian has purely imaginary eigenvalues, linearization alone is not sufficient to classify the equilibrium. In such cases, further analysis beyond linear approximation is required." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d168b25-7771-5e55-8be8-2eb16080b9b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14496 :tutorial/title "A Note About Stable Centers"} :step/id #uuid "d039d8e2-81e7-5160-bcfd-7765a694f63a" :step/math-academy-id 591492}] :activity/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221470}]}
